Guiding Philosophy: A Communicative Approach
Both curricula state that grammar should be:

Taught in context: Learned through reading and writing texts, not just memorising rules.

Functional: Understanding how to use a structure to achieve a purpose (e.g., using the present perfect to introduce your experiences in a biography).

Developmental: Errors are part of learning; the focus is on improving clarity and range over time.

Hong Kong: EDB English Language Curriculum Guide (Secondary 1-6) - Language Dimensions
The EDB provides a Learning Progression Framework (LPF) that outlines developing competency. It's less a strict checklist and more a continuum.

S1-S3 (Key Stage 3 Level) - Foundation & Consolidation
Sentence Structures: Moving from simple to compound and complex sentences.

S1 Focus: Mastery of simple present, past, future; subject-verb agreement; basic questions and negatives.

*S2-S3 Focus:* Introduction of adverbial clauses (time, reason, condition - e.g., When he arrived..., Because it rained..., If you study...), relative clauses (who, which, that), and varied sentence openings.

Verb Tenses & Forms:

Core tenses: Present simple/continuous, past simple/continuous, future (will, be going to).

Introduction of present perfect tense (for experiences) and modals (can, could, should, must, have to) for expressing ability, advice, obligation.

Grammar Items:

Countable/uncountable nouns & quantifiers (some, any, much, many, a lot of).

Comparatives & superlatives.

Prepositions of time, place, and movement (at, on, in, to, from, into).

Basic reported speech (statements, e.g., He said he was tired.).

Text Awareness: Linking ideas with basic connectors (and, but, or, so, because, first, next, finally).

S4-S6 (Key Stage 4 / DSE Level) - Complexity & Accuracy for Academic/Exam Use
Sentence Structures: Mastery of complex sentences for argumentation and analysis.

Use of participle phrases (Having considered the arguments, I believe...), conditional sentences (including Type 3: If they had invested, they would have saved...), and inversion for emphasis (Not only does it cause pollution, but it also...).

Verb Tenses & Forms:

Full range of tenses for precision: past perfect, future continuous/perfect.

Advanced modals (might, ought to, could have done) for hedging and speculating.

Active vs. Passive Voice used strategically (especially in academic/science writing).

Grammar Items:

Reported speech for all sentence types (questions, commands).

Phrasal verbs and prepositional combinations.

Articles (a, an, the) for nuanced meaning.

Unreal past after 'wish' and 'if only' (I wish I had more time).

Textual Competence: Using a wide range of cohesive devices (Furthermore, Nevertheless, In contrast, As a result, In conclusion) to structure essays and arguments logically.

UK: National Curriculum for English (KS3 & KS4) - Language Guidance
The UK curriculum is even less prescriptive on grammar lists, but the end-of-stage expectations are clear.

Key Stage 3 (End of Year 9 Expectation)
Pupils should be able to:

Write accurately, fluently and at length.

Apply grammatical knowledge (e.g., of tense, modal verbs, passive voice) to improve writing.

Use a range of sentence structures for clarity, purpose and effect, including the rhetorical question.

Maintain correct subject-verb agreement with complex subjects.

Use the full range of punctuation correctly: commas for clarity, colons to introduce lists, semicolons to link related clauses, apostrophes for possession and contraction.

Use Standard English confidently in formal writing and speech.

Key Stage 4 (GCSE Level - End of Year 11)
This is assessed directly in exams. Students must demonstrate control in:

Sentence Demarcation: Flawless use of full stops, capital letters, question and exclamation marks.

Sophisticated Punctuation: Accurate use of colons, semicolons, parentheses (dashes/brackets/commas), and ellipsis for effect.

Grammatical Precision: Consistent and accurate use of:

Verb forms and tense for clarity across narratives and arguments.

Pronoun reference (clear antecedents).

Prepositions.

Stylistic Grammar for Effect: Conscious use of:

Sentence variety (minor, simple, compound, complex) to control pace and emphasis.

Figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification).

Rhetorical devices (tripling, anaphora) in writing and speech.

Synthesis: The "Hidden" Grammar Curriculum in Your Course Design
You should integrate these points. Don't call a lesson "Prepositions," but design tasks that require them.

Example for S1 Course (Theme: "My Neighbourhood - The World Around Us")

Task: Create a podcast giving a tour of your area.

Integrated Language Focus:

Prepositions of place: "Next to the park, opposite the MTR, behind the shopping centre."

There is/There are: "There is a great wet market. There are many old shops."

Present simple for facts: "It opens at 7 am. People buy fresh food."

Example for S5 DSE Course (Theme: "Technology & Ethics")

Task: Write a formal letter to a tech CEO arguing for better data privacy.

Integrated Language Focus:

Complex sentences with concession: "Although your service is popular, it often exploits user data."

Modal verbs for recommendation: "The company should implement...", "You must consider..."

Passive voice for objectivity: "Data is collected without clear consent."

Cohesive devices for argument flow: "Furthermore,... However,... Consequently,..."

Actionable Checklist for Your Curriculum:
Map grammar to themes: What language structures are needed to complete the task? (e.g., a "school trip itinerary" task requires future tense and prepositions of time).

Use "Focus on Form": After a communicative task, highlight a common error or introduce a more advanced structure to "upgrade" their work.

Prioritise high-leverage grammar: For DSE, spend time on articles, preposition collocations (impact on, concern about), and sentence connectors—these are common error hotspots that affect band scores.

Provide models: Show excellent sample answers and annotate the grammar and sentence structures used to achieve a high mark.

This integrated approach ensures students see language as a tool for communication, not just a set of rules, making it more engaging and effective for exam and real-world success.